1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
 Okay, let's start talking about the public sector. We will focus on monetary criteria only.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,000
 What are the differences between public and private sector projects?

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,000
 Well, I summarized some main differences in this table.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,000
 Usually public sector projects are larger.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:27,000
 They have a longer life, several decades or no exception.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:38,000
 While in the private sector we usually have shorter project lives like 2 years, maybe 5 or 10 years, 25 years is already exceptional.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:47,000
 Public sector, we are not talking about profits, we will be talking about cost benefits and disbenefits.

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,000
 While in the private sector we have revenues and costs.

9
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:57,000
 Funding system is different, interest rate is usually lower in the public sector than in the private sector.

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:03,000
 Public sector is more inclined to use multi-criteria analysis than the private sector.

11
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:10,000
 Private sector is primarily economically concerned, commercial organizations.

12
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
 While in the public sector politics might play a role.

13
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,000
 In between the public and private sector we have public-private partnerships.

14
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 What are public sector projects? Well, you see here a number of examples.

15
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:39,000
 Building hospitals and clinics, parks, utilities, schools, economic development, convention centers, public housing, emergency relief, transportation and so on.

16
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,000
 I talked about cost benefits and disbenefits.

17
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:53,000
 While costs, that's everything that the government entity has to pay for construction and operations and maintenance of the project.

18
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
 The benefits is what the owners, the public are experiencing.

19
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,000
 And disbenefits, well it's also something that the public experiences.

20
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:14,000
 Usually it's another group. It's an undesirable, unplanned, negative consequence of the project.

21
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:20,000
 An example could be of a disbenefit if you build a home.

22
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:28,000
 It could have disbenefits for your neighbors. You do not plan that of course.

23
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:35,000
 But it's not to be avoided that there will be some noise, that there will be trucks driving up and down.

24
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
 So it will be a nuisance for your neighbors.

25
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:56,000
 Having these different parties also means that we will have to tell beforehand from which viewpoint that we will do the cost benefit analysis.

26
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
 Another example in the grey frame.

27
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:11,000
 A flood control project will have a first cost of 1.4 million with an annual maintenance cost of 40,000 and a 10 year life.

28
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,000
 Reduced flood damage is expected to amount to 175,000 per year.

29
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:21,000
 Loss income to farmers is estimated to be 25,000 per year.

30
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,000
 So at an interest rate of 6% should the project be undertaken.

31
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,000
 So that lost income to the farmers definitely is not planned.

32
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
 It's not as if we don't want that. But it's there. It's unavoidable.

33
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:46,000
 Of course often, and that happens more and more, the public sector government is working together with private contractors.

34
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,000
 And there are different formulas to do so.

35
00:03:50,000 --> 00:04:00,000
 If the contractors do not share project risk, they are either paid on a fixed price basis or on a cost reimbursable basis.

36
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
 More and more we see the PPP where contractors share in project risks.

37
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:15,000
 It's the public private partnerships with typical design, build or design, build, operate, maintain, finance projects.

38
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:21,000
 It's a very interesting topic, but it's a bit out of scope for this course.

39
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:28,000
 Important also, the public sector, of course, companies need to behave ethically.

40
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:33,000
 But ethics are even more important maybe for the government.

41
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,000
 The government is making choices with their project.

42
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,000
 It's always aimed at a target group.

43
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:47,000
 They have to make sure that they are fair, that they do not forget minorities, for instance.

44
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,000
 So they have to be fair to the whole community.

45
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
 They have to think about the time impact.

46
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:04,000
 Maybe it's looking good for the moment financially speaking, but future generations will have to pay a lot of money in order to keep up the project.

47
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:14,000
 Should we only look at money? Maybe we should look at environmental impact, for instance.

48
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:18,000
 So these considerations are pretty important.

49
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:26,000
 By the way, we already briefly mentioned ethics, but then more company ethics in the first chapter.

50
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,000
 A public sector example.

51
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:50,000
 So we're dealing with a project where the intended investment is a purchase of land, more specifically green-built flood plain land, in order to preserve low-laying green areas in wildlife habitat east of the city of Dundee.

52
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:53,000
 Not everybody is happy with the project.

53
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:00,000
 For instance, developers oppose because the project will decrease the amount of available land for commercial development.

54
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,000
 The city engineer and the economic development director have collected some data.

55
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
 Obviously, at this stage, there are only rough estimates.

56
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:12,000
 In the table, you find a list of these estimates.

57
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:17,000
 The horizon they are thinking of is, as you can see in the table, 15 years.

58
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:27,000
 And the question is to identify the three different viewpoints and for each of those to label the estimates as cost benefits or disbenefits.

59
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:32,000
 So I summarized it in this table.

60
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:39,000
 What you find in this column is what is given in the table.

61
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:42,000
 What are the points of view that we need to take into account?

62
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:47,000
 We need to take into account the citizen, the city, the city budget, economic development.

63
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:53,000
 So what are the objectives of these stakeholder groups?

64
00:06:53,000 --> 00:07:02,000
 Well, the citizen wants to maximize quality and wellness of themselves, their family, their neighborhoods.

65
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:03,000
 The city budget.

66
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:04,000
 The city budget.

67
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:19,000
 Well, it's important to have a balanced budget and to have a sufficient budget for the rapidly growing city services and economic development has to promote or wants to promote industrial and commercial development.

68
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:28,000
 So and then, well, here you see doesn't matter the costs or costs.

69
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,000
 Here we have benefits.

70
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:35,000
 Also similar.

71
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:51,000
 But here we have some some disagreements, for instance, the annual loss commercially is a disadvantage for the city and the city budget, but is considered a cost for the economic development.

72
00:07:51,000 --> 00:08:03,000
 And while the loss of sales tax rebates is a disadvantage for the citizen, but is a cost for the city budget and economic development.

73
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,000
 So what is the lesson learned here?

74
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:17,000
 The lesson learned here is that before you start doing your cost benefit analysis, you should pick a stakeholder and say, I'm going to do the analysis here because the definition of cost benefit,

75
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:31,000
 this benefit, not the definition, but the classification you make of the different cost elements and benefit elements can be different according to the point of view that you're taking.

76
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:37,000
 And here the point of view can be the citizen, the city or the economic development.

77
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
 Now benefit cost analysis.

78
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:43,000
 Well, we have a benefit cost ratio.

79
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:47,000
 Basically, it is the present word or the annual word or the future word.

80
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:50,000
 And we have benefits over costs.

81
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
 Pretty straightforward.

82
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
 Now.

83
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:05,000
 There's one thing you should realize that is that there are different definitions and the conventional definition is to put.

84
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:13,000
 The ratio benefit cost as the benefits minus the disbenefits over the costs.

85
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,000
 There's also a modified one.

86
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:26,000
 And here we have the benefits minus the disbenefits minus the maintenance operating costs over the initial cost.

87
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:27,000
 So there are two different definitions.

88
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
 Is one better than the other?

89
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
 I wouldn't say yes or no.

90
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:36,000
 You just have to be careful what is asked for or what you find in papers.

91
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,000
 Maybe you note the benefit cost ratio.

92
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:44,000
 I am sure that we briefly mentioned it when we talked about the criteria.

93
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:48,000
 In fact, it's like the profitability index.

94
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:50,000
 This formula is the present word.

95
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:57,000
 In fact, we have our initial cost and then we have the other cash flows, the future cash flows.

96
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:00,000
 And we want the present word to be larger than zero.

97
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:07,000
 Or we could say that we make a ratio of it and we say that it should be larger than one.

98
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:11,000
 And in fact, this is our benefit cost ratio.

99
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:14,000
 OK, so it's not something entirely new.

100
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:20,000
 OK, let's have a look at the example for a single project.

101
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:29,000
 So the Ford Foundation expects to award 15 million in grants to public high schools to improve teaching and engineering related subjects.

102
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
 So that's initial investment, the cost.

103
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,000
 These grants will extend over a period of 10 years.

104
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:41,000
 An estimated savings of 1.5 million per year in faculty salaries and student related expenses as expected.

105
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:44,000
 So that are our benefits.

106
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:53,000
 Further, an operating cost of 500,000 dollars per year will be needed to make this new grant project successful.

107
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:54,000
 So that's our that's a cost.

108
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,000
 In fact, that's our maintenance and operation cost, you would say.

109
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:04,000
 But also an estimated 200,000 per year will be removed from other funding initiatives.

110
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
 That's a disadvantage.

111
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:15,000
 The project of allocating grants was not meant to remove.

112
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:16,000
 Well, it's initial.

113
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:23,000
 It's what we say its main objective was not to remove those funds, but it's a negative consequence of it.

114
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:31,000
 OK, so we have benefits, disbenefits and costs.

115
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
 These are given here.

116
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:36,000
 Well, we have to make an annual worth of it.

117
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:42,000
 So we have our initial amount times the AP factor 6 percent 10.

118
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
 Then we have here 2 million.

119
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:57,000
 Now, then if we use the two definitions, if we use the conventional benefit cost ratio, we have a 0.51 outcome.

120
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:13,000
 If we use the modified benefit cost ratio in which we also put the maintenance and operation costs in the numerator, we have 0.39.

121
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:23,000
 Of course, we could also simply look at the sum and see whether benefit costs are larger than zero or not.

122
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:33,000
 And what we conclude from all three instances is that it's not a good project.

123
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:37,000
 OK, these are some real life examples.

124
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
 Regional traffic signal program.

125
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:50,000
 And you see that here it's evaluated with a benefit cost ratio, which seems to be tremendously high.

126
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:53,000
 So it's a very interesting project.

127
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:59,000
 Online you find all kinds of benefit costs compendia here for road weather management.

128
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:02,000
 So you can find quite a lot for free on the Internet.

129
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
 But that's just an example.

130
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:08,000
 We're not going further in that area.

131
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:14,000
 What if we don't have if we do not have one, but we have two alternatives?

132
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:20,000
 Well, then we have to do an incremental benefit cost analysis.

133
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:27,000
 Instead of reading through this procedure, it might be more interesting to have an example.

134
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:35,000
 Of course, you're expected to know how to what the procedure is, and you have to be capable of applying it.

135
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:42,000
 But instead of reading it, let's jump to the example.

136
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:49,000
 So here we are talking about the project to construct a new patient room wing to the municipal hospital.

137
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:55,000
 And there are two proposed designs labeled A and B, of which one needs to be chosen.

138
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,000
 So do nothing is not an alternative here.

139
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:06,000
 The design do not differ in some cost categories, but they do differ in the elements that are given in the table.

140
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:10,000
 Construction cost and building maintenance costs.

141
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
 It's clear what they mean.

142
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:20,000
 Patient usage cost is about the amount patients have to pay over the insurance coverage allowed for a hospital room.

143
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:29,000
 The discount rate is five percent and the life of the building and does the project horizon to be considered is 30 years.

144
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:38,000
 Then the first question is to use a conventional benefit cost ratio to select design A or B.

145
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:45,000
 So remember conventional means benefits minus these benefits over costs.

146
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:46,000
 So all costs.

147
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:50,000
 And then there's a second question that adds some complexity.

148
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:58,000
 A privately owned hospital in a nearby city lodged a complaint against design A because they fear a loss in income.

149
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:12,000
 Of five hundred thousand because they surgery patients will not come to them anymore, but go to the new hospital wing and design B also has some opponents.

150
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:25,000
 The merchants of the city are not happy because the hospital wing would eliminate the parking lot and they fear a loss of income of four hundred thousand from shoppers that will not be there anymore.

151
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:29,000
 So the question is, can you please redo the business?

152
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:34,000
 Sorry, the benefit cost analysis taking these benefits into account.

153
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:39,000
 OK, let's have a look.

154
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:45,000
 First thing that we have to do, of course, before we can proceed is we have to.

155
00:15:45,000 --> 00:16:00,000
 We have to make sure that we have an annual word here, so we have to make sure that we have the right numbers here.

156
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:04,000
 I think by now you're very good already in computing annual words.

157
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:12,000
 Incremental, so we mean B minus A because B is the larger cost alternative.

158
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
 And here this is our benefits.

159
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:23,000
 So in for we have two hundred and fifty thousand minus three hundred and forty five thousand two hundred and fifty.

160
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
 So that means that if you're.

161
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:31,000
 Only considering costs and benefits, we will choose option A.

162
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:36,000
 Now, if you want to take into account also the this benefits, which was our second question.

163
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:49,000
 Then we see that we have to take into account the concerns about the concerns raised by the other hospital, by the commercial people.

164
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
 And then we see that we have.

165
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:58,000
 An incremental this benefit of minus one hundred thousand.

166
00:16:58,000 --> 00:16:59,000
 So if you fill that out in our.

167
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:08,000
 In our formula, we have one point one.

168
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:14,000
 So that means that we will go for the more expensive alternative while here we were staying with.

169
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:17,000
 Lower cost one.

170
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:20,000
 Again, it illustrates the fact that.

171
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:23,000
 It's important to.

172
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:32,000
 To decide whether you put what point of view you will take, whether you will include these benefits or not.

173
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:36,000
 And it's also a nice illustration of how to do an incremental analysis.

174
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:45,000
 If you have multiple alternatives, well, we do pairwise.

175
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:46,000
 Benefit cost analysis.

176
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:50,000
 Well, pairwise incremental benefit cost analysis.

177
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:54,000
 It's not very different from what we did.

178
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:58,000
 In the rate of return chapter.

179
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
 So, OK, let's have a look at this example.

180
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:08,000
 So we have a project here, project of the Economic Development Corporation.

181
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:11,000
 Project of the Economic Development Corporation.

182
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:17,000
 Abbreviation EDC, which is a nonprofit corporation in Florida.

183
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:22,000
 The Intended Investment Project concerns a major recreational water park.

184
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:27,000
 A request for a proposal has been sent out and four proposals were received.

185
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
 One of those needs to be selected.

186
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:37,000
 The proposals differ in size and details of the water park attractions proposed.

187
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:45,000
 Interest rate to take into account is 7 percent and a study period of eight years is recommended.

188
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
 Some extra information.

189
00:18:50,000 --> 00:19:02,000
 So there are guidelines that the entertainment industry can receive up to five hundred thousand as a first year incentive award and a 10 percent of that amount each year for eight years in property tax reduction.

190
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:10,000
 Each proposal includes a provision that residents of the city and even the county will benefit from reduced entrance fees.

191
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:16,000
 This fee reduction will be in effect as long as a property tax reduction incentive continues.

192
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:29,000
 EDC has estimated the total annual entrance fees with reduction for locals included and also they estimated the extra sales tax revenue expected for each proposal.

193
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:39,000
 The data can be found in the table and the question is please conduct an incremental benefit cost analysis to select the best alternative.

194
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:49,000
 So as we have seen before, this is the data and here we already have the computations.

195
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:51,000
 Let's have a look.

196
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
 The viewpoint of the citizen is taken.

197
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:58,000
 We have to make sure that we are comparing things that we can compare.

198
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:02,000
 We will first convert this into annual words.

199
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:20,000
 So for instance here we have 250,000 times AP factor 7 percent eight years plus 25,000 gives us 66,000 and for each of the proposals we are doing the same thing.

200
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:22,000
 They are ordered.

201
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:23,000
 Then we have to compare.

202
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:25,000
 So we compare the alternatives.

203
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:29,000
 We have to compute delta C.

204
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:37,000
 Delta C is 26,000, 93,000 minus 66,000.

205
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:45,000
 Then we have to compute the incremental benefits.

206
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:52,000
 So here the difference is 50,000.

207
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
 Here the difference is 10,000.

208
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
 So together they make 60,000.

209
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:07,000
 And then we have our benefit cost ratio of 2.24.

210
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
 So that means the increment is justified.

211
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:14,000
 So at this point we will select proposal 2.

212
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:28,000
 If we repeat the same thing, we see that if we compare 3 to 2, we see that we have a ratio of 0.62.

213
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:32,000
 So that means no, we are not going for proposal 3.

214
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
 So we still stick with proposal 2 as being the best.

215
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:42,000
 We repeat the calculations and here we see it's 1.3.

216
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:51,000
 So proposal 4 seems to be the best, but it's not really complying with the guidelines.

217
00:21:52,000 --> 00:22:00,000
 It's saying that first 500,000 that was put forward, 800,000 is more.

218
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
 So we should reconsider.

219
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:13,000
 Either we are saying we are not going for 4 or we should reconsider that initial budget of 500,000 and raise it to 800,000.

220
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:18,000
 And here you have the full solution written out for you.

